From c146911d8fb5b122da53d74ec46b295ae70c7e79 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jared Wyles <jared.wyles@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 15:55:33 +1100
Subject: [PATCH 35/35] tests: check row-list attention over the lists a
 unified KV pool produces

test-backend-ops compared row-list attention on a GPU backend with the CPU
only for lists of scattered cells with padding at the tail. A pool that keeps
a sequence's cells together produces a different shape: runs of consecutive
cells laid out in blocks of 32 entries, each run's last block ending in
padding. Metal reads such a block straight from the cache, and compiles the
gather out of the kernel when every block of every list qualifies, so the
paths most decode steps take had no backend-level check.

test_flash_attn_ext_kv_rows_runs adds four layouts over the same op:

  consecutive        one range filling whole blocks (the gather-free kernel)
  runs_padded        several runs, padding inside the list (gather-free)
  run_at_cache_end   a padded run in the cache's last cells, which a blind
                     whole-block read would overrun (the general kernel)
  mixed              consecutive and scattered blocks in one list

On Metal builds the row-list cases run as the ctest
skippy_backend_ops_kv_rows, so the project's native test run includes them.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.2 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01QA3dWey3RBtBgDz1Tery9Y
